WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:10.127
Sustainable transportation supports present mobility and access without undermining environmental, economic, and social well-being over time.

2
00:00:10.127 --> 00:00:15.326
The source chapter organizes sustainability around those three connected dimensions.

3
00:00:15.326 --> 00:00:20.958
A project that lowers emissions but is unaffordable, unsafe, or inaccessible is incomplete.

4
00:00:20.958 --> 00:00:28.385
A project that produces short-term travel savings but creates large maintenance or climate costs shifts burdens forward.

5
00:00:28.385 --> 00:00:39.402
Transportation affects greenhouse-gas emissions, local air quality, noise, land consumption, water, habitat, safety, household budgets, public finance, and access to opportunity.

6
00:00:39.402 --> 00:00:46.829
Some effects occur during construction, others during operation, and others through induced land-use and travel changes.

7
00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:52.089
A lifecycle view prevents a narrow operating metric from hiding the full consequence.

8
00:00:52.089 --> 00:00:54.627
Indicators translate goals into measures.

9
00:00:54.627 --> 00:01:06.015
Useful indicators can include emissions, energy, severe collisions, accessibility, reliability, mode share, household transportation cost, system condition, and exposure by population.

10
00:01:06.015 --> 00:01:10.347
Each needs a definition, baseline, geography, data source, and target.

11
00:01:10.347 --> 00:01:15.670
A dashboard without decision rules can describe a problem without changing investment.

12
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:26.315
Sustainable mobility also includes cleaner vehicles and fuels, but vehicle technology alone does not address distance, land use, congestion, road danger, or unequal access.

13
00:01:26.315 --> 00:01:33.433
Demand management, transit, active transportation, pricing, and compact development can complement decarbonization.

14
00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:34.747
Torrance application.

15
00:01:34.747 --> 00:01:38.378
This local section is separate from the source adaptation.

16
00:01:38.378 --> 00:01:44.825
It cites the Torrance Multi-Use Active Transportation Trail project page and the Torrance General Plan.

17
00:01:44.825 --> 00:02:00.472
The City describes the trail concept as a connection between the Regional Transit Center and Downtown Torrance that could support walking, bicycling, rolling, access to destinations, reduced vehicle miles traveled, and lower greenhouse-gas emissions.

18
00:02:00.472 --> 00:02:06.544
A sustainability evaluation would establish a baseline and avoid counting aspirations as results.

19
00:02:06.544 --> 00:02:18.123
It could measure completed-network connectivity, safe crossings, use by time and mode, access to jobs and transit, serious collisions, maintenance, shade, and changes in vehicle travel.

20
00:02:18.123 --> 00:02:25.196
It should examine who can use the facility and whether benefits reach people with limited transportation choices.

21
00:02:25.196 --> 00:02:34.397
Construction materials, right-of-way constraints, ongoing upkeep, and interactions with freight or adjacent properties also belong in the analysis.

22
00:02:34.397 --> 00:02:40.781
If measured use differs from the forecast, the City could adjust connections, operations, or outreach.

23
00:02:40.781 --> 00:02:43.660
This is not a benefit finding for the project.

24
00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:49.043
It is an example of converting broad sustainability language into testable indicators.

25
00:02:49.043 --> 00:03:00.121
This episode adapts chapter nine of Transportation Policies, Programs and History by Audirac, Raley, Reiner, and Sharifi-Asl, published by Mavs Open Press under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.

26
00:03:00.121 --> 00:03:02.062
Third-party items are excluded.

27
00:03:02.062 --> 00:03:04.691
City sources are independently summarized.

28
